Let me carefully structure the full design.

Main structure:

1) Rationale — hypotheses H1 (main effect of SSP: SSP present < SSP absent ASR), H2 (interaction: SSP is more effective against prompt-level than token-level, or vice versa; the rationale that token-level suffixes are optimized to bypass safety fine-tuning through near-adversarial token patterns, whereas SSP operates at the instruction/context level, so SSP should better mitigate prompt-level attacks that work via instruction hijacking; token-level GCG suffixes are somewhat decoding/context insensitive — indeed, the literature (e.g., the GCG paper) shows adversarial suffixes transfer across system prompts; SSP may have limited effect. The 2x2 interaction answers the real question).

